{"name":"Nirvana","hex":"#a2919b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nirvana","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Complete cessation of dukkha; a blissful state attained through realization of sunyata; enlightened experience.","State of paradise; heightened or great pleasure or peace; a state of universal perfection and bliss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nirvana"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nirvana","blocks":[{"text":"Nirvana, in the Indian religions, is the concept of an individual's passions being extinguished as the ultimate state of salvation, release, or liberation from suffering (duḥkha) and from the cycle of birth and rebirth (saṃsāra)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nirvana","wikidata":"Q42927"},{"title":"Nirvana (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Nirvana was an American rock band formed in Aberdeen, Washington, in 1987. Founded by lead singer and guitarist Kurt Cobain and bassist Krist Novoselic, the band went through a succession of drummers, most notably Chad Channing, before recruiting Dave Grohl in 1990. Nirvana's success popularized alternative rock, and they were often referenced as the flagship band of Generation X. Their music maintains a popular following and continues to influence rock culture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Nirvana_around_1992.jpg/330px-Nirvana_around_1992.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nirvana_(band)","wikidata":"Q11649"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Gautama_Buddha_gains_nirvana.jpg/500px-Gautama_Buddha_gains_nirvana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Gautama_Buddha_gains_nirvana.jpg/1280px-Gautama_Buddha_gains_nirvana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.